Here is a complete, automated setup script (setup_agv.sh) that will handle everything for you: it updates your Raspberry Pi 5, installs all required Python and system libraries (OpenCV, pyserial, numpy, RPi.GPIO), enables the hardware UART serial port, creates your project folder, and sets up a background service so your car starts automatically the exact second the Pi boots up.   
main_vision.py

Step 1: Create the Automated Setup Script
On your Raspberry Pi 5, open a terminal and create a new script file:

Bash


nano setup_agv.sh
Paste the following complete bash script into the file:

Bash


#!/bin/bash
# ==============================================================================
# AGV Automated Installer & Auto-Start Configurator for Raspberry Pi 5
# ==============================================================================

echo "=== [1/5] Updating System Packages & Installing Dependencies ==="
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y python3-pip python3-opencv python3-serial python3-numpy python3-rpi.gpio

echo "=== [2/5] Enabling Hardware UART (GPIO 14 & 15) ==="
CONFIG_FILE="/boot/firmware/config.txt"
if [ ! -f "$CONFIG_FILE" ]; then
    CONFIG_FILE="/boot/config.txt"
fi

if grep -q "dtparam=uart0=on" "$CONFIG_FILE"; then
    echo "UART0 is already enabled."
else
    echo "dtparam=uart0=on" | sudo tee -a "$CONFIG_FILE"
    echo "UART0 added to $CONFIG_FILE"
fi

echo "=== [3/5] Creating Project Directory ==="
PROJECT_DIR="/home/pi/agv_project"
mkdir -p "$PROJECT_DIR"
chown -R pi:pi "$PROJECT_DIR"

echo "=== [4/5] Creating Systemd Service for Auto-Start ==="
SERVICE_FILE="/etc/systemd/system/agv.service"
sudo bash -c "cat > $SERVICE_FILE" <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=AGV Autonomous Vision Control Service
After=multi-user.target serial-getty@ttyAMA0.service

[Service]
Type=simple
User=pi
WorkingDirectory=$PROJECT_DIR
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 $PROJECT_DIR/main_vision.py --port /dev/ttyAMA0 --baud 115200
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=3

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

echo "=== [5/5] Registering & Enabling the Auto-Start Service ==="
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable agv.service

echo ""
echo "===================================================================="
echo " Setup complete successfully!"
echo " Next step: Place your 'main_vision.py' file inside: $PROJECT_DIR"
echo " Then reboot your Raspberry Pi 5 using: sudo reboot"
echo "===================================================================="
Save and exit the editor by pressing Ctrl + O, then Enter, and Ctrl + X.

Step 2: Run the Installer Script
Make the installer script executable and run it with root privileges:

Bash


chmod +x setup_agv.sh
sudo ./setup_agv.sh
Step 3: Put Your Code in Place
Make sure your finalized vision script is named main_vision.py and placed inside the project folder created by the script:

Bash


nano /home/pi/agv_project/main_vision.py
(Paste your python code here, save, and exit).

Step 4: Reboot and Let It Run!
Reboot your Raspberry Pi 5:

Bash


sudo reboot
What Happens Next?
The Pi 5 powers on and boots up.

The agv.service background script launches automatically.   
main_vision.py

It opens communication with your ESP32-S3 over /dev/ttyAMA0 at 115200 baud, reads the camera feed, executes your PD lane-keeping and traffic-light algorithms, and runs your car entirely on its own with zero laptop or manual terminal initialization required!   
